WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:13.340
One of the most surprising things about Japan
– and there are many surprising things – is

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:14.740
how safe it is.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.840
See, Japan is one of the few countries in
the world where you can leave an iPhone X

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.430
[Ten] in a coffee shop and not have to worry
about anything but dropping by the next day.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:27.420
Because, my friends, it is almost certain
that your recently released, brand new iPhone

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.340
X will be there waiting for you.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.640
And no, I'm not exaggerating.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:40.780
Also, Japan, is just a beautiful country with
amazing sites, where everything seems to be

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.100
where it should be.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.019
But...

00:00:49.019 --> 00:00:54.260
As you know this isn’t a travel channel...
and this video isn’t sponsored by Japan’s

00:00:54.260 --> 00:01:01.860
Ministry of Tourism, so... let’s get to
the point.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.830
Recently we found out that Emperor Akihito
has decided to abdicate.

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:11.050
(“The emperor of Japan will step down on
April 30, 2019, the first abdication by a

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:12.810
Japanese monarch in two centuries”.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:13.810
New York Times.)

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:18.079
Well, see: this emperor came to the throne
almost 30 years ago, in 1989...

00:01:18.079 --> 00:01:21.399
Just when the great Japanese crisis began.

00:01:21.399 --> 00:01:26.650
No, in Japan the emperor has no power, so
Akihito wasn’t responsible, but don’t

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:29.969
say that can’t be just coincidence and bad
luck.

00:01:29.969 --> 00:01:35.200
We can practically say that it was after Akihito’s
arrival that 30 years of stagnation began.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.909
In this video we will see what happened, what
caused this great crisis and why the German

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:41.900
economic model has managed to defeat the Japanese
model.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:46.929
But first, my friends, a small history lesson.

00:01:46.929 --> 00:01:52.149
(MIRACLES EXIST)

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:59.060
Japan and Germany are two very interesting
countries, with a lot in common and, also,

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.060
many differences.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:03.450
After the Second World War both countries
were literally in ruins.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:07.929
They had lost not only their infrastructure,
but also an entire generation.

00:02:07.929 --> 00:02:13.370
However, despite this, between the 1950s and
the 1970s, both countries experienced such

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:18.490
a powerful takeoff that in those two decades
they not only managed to recover, but to become

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:24.100
two of the largest economies in the world,
as well as two of the richest countries.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:29.470
In fact, even though it might seem incredible
today, for years it was speculated that Japan

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:34.390
could surpass even the United States as the
world's largest economy.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:38.010
How things have changed!

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:44.900
The fact is that between 1955 and 1973 Japan
grew at an annual average rate of 9%, and

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.790
between 1973 and 1990, above 4%.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:51.030
These truly are some incredible rates.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:56.750
And Germany, didn’t stay behind, it had
average annual growth of 7% for almost 30

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:57.750
years.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.270
Both countries were at full throttle.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:03.490
Their factories worked tirelessly, there was
no unemployment, and both Germans and the

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:07.120
Japanese saved and invested their money all
over the world.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:15.680
Suddenly, Japanese and German companies became
famous worldwide.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:21.210
This was very important, especially for Japan,
a country that lives constantly under nature’s

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:22.840
threat...

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:27.720
Besides being a very mountainous country,
Japan suffers from all kinds of natural disasters:

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.480
earthquakes, cyclones, tsunamis, etc, etc.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:39.340
By the way, in future VISUALPOLITIK videos,
we will tell you all about how these two "economic

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.460
miracles" took place.

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:49.320
Well, the fact is that in 1989 this entire
economic takeoff stopped, it came to an end

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.790
and gave way to what is now known as the great
Japanese stagnation.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:57.740
(“Forget Greece, Japan is the world's real
economic time bomb” Christopher Matthews,

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:58.780
an Axios reporter.)

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:02.800
I’m convinced, this isn’t news to you,
as it isn’t strange to hear people talk

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.710
about Japan along with the word crisis.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:11.160
Yes, ever since I can recall, Japan has been
the country of eternal crisis, the country

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.260
that has one good year for every three bad
ones.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:19.289
In fact, nowadays we can easily declare Germany
the winner in the growth race that both countries

00:04:19.289 --> 00:04:23.759
maintained for decades.

00:04:23.759 --> 00:04:29.520
See, today Germany – which has also gone
through some bad times in recent decades – is

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.760
richer, has healthier public accounts, is
more competitive internationally and has higher

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:34.760
wages.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:39.740
To get an idea of Japan’s situation, I think
we can use two very revealing facts.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:44.560
First, in Japan real wages, taking inflation
into account, are lower today than salaries

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.639
back in 1990, 30 years ago!

00:04:47.639 --> 00:04:52.919
And second, the Japanese government’s public
debt is the highest in the world: 250% of

00:04:52.919 --> 00:05:01.659
the GDP, yes 250%, you heard that right.

00:05:01.659 --> 00:05:03.939
So my friends, you might be wondering, what
the hell happened?

00:05:03.939 --> 00:05:07.520
What made the German model so much better
than the Japanese model?

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.639
Well... obviously, there are lots of reasons,
but we believe that 3 of them are, by far,

00:05:12.639 --> 00:05:15.139
the most important.

00:05:15.139 --> 00:05:20.129
Let's take a look at them.

00:05:20.129 --> 00:05:23.090
(A PROBLEMATIC MODEL)

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:29.050
First, we can say that there was a big difference
between Japan and Germany’s development.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:33.960
The country of the rising sun, opted to protect
large industrial conglomerates, the 'Keiretsu'.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.770
The keiretsu are, so to speak, holdings that
do practically everything, that is, they have

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:45.349
companies for all kinds of things: cars, planes,
tires, toothpaste...

00:05:45.349 --> 00:05:49.130
Everything is manufactured by the same holding
company, whose companies support each other,

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:55.909
buy and sell products between them and even
have their own banks to finance themselves.

00:05:55.909 --> 00:05:57.560
So what’s the problem with this?

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:04.150
Well... these large conglomerates tended to
be very bureaucratic and not very flexible.

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.930
So decision-making was a difficult and very
slow process.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:07.930
But...

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:11.550
The Japanese government decided to compensate
for all these disadvantages, and didn’t

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:15.389
hesitate to give the keiretsu all kinds of
advantages and privileges.

00:06:15.389 --> 00:06:19.889
For example, Japan’s Central Bank financed
them generously at abnormally low rates through

00:06:19.889 --> 00:06:20.889
specific programs.

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:25.159
However, my friends, the results weren’t
at all good.

00:06:25.159 --> 00:06:29.169
See, these large groups became very influential
in the country’s politics;

00:06:29.169 --> 00:06:32.529
they hid many problems, because of course,
when one of these conglomerates’ companies

00:06:32.529 --> 00:06:36.029
did badly, it was extremely easy for them
to infect the rest;

00:06:36.029 --> 00:06:39.210
they were in a lot of debt; and they even
limited competition.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:43.960
Of course, how can one compete with such groups,
if they are also protected by the government

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:45.949
and have all kinds of privileges?

00:06:45.949 --> 00:06:47.779
Sounds hard, right?

00:06:47.779 --> 00:06:51.479
On the other hand, in Germany, the political
commitment was completely different and focused

00:06:51.479 --> 00:06:57.169
on creating a very competitive market with
small and medium sized companies.

00:06:57.169 --> 00:07:04.590
But, are there large industrial groups in
Germany?

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:09.000
Of course, and that's a good thing, as long
as they don’t depend on the government or

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.710
receive privileges that impede competition.

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:16.069
So while Germany’s companies thrived, Japan’s
large conglomerates have had many problems,

00:07:16.069 --> 00:07:20.080
and most have disappeared or broken down at
some point.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:24.919
But that’s not all...

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:27.919
(THE DISCOUNT POLICY)

00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:34.349
For years, Japanese politicians thought that
in order to export more, devaluing the currency

00:07:34.349 --> 00:07:36.770
from time to time... wasn’t a bad thing.

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:40.080
However, in 1985, the real problems began.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:46.090
See, as a result of all these currency wars,
the United States gained a fairly large external

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:47.090
deficit.

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:50.800
And because Washington politicians of the
time, as they do now, didn’t like it, they

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:55.490
forced the so-called "Plaza Accord", an agreement
between the United States, Japan, Germany,

00:07:55.490 --> 00:08:01.020
France and the United Kingdom to depreciate
the dollar to decrease this trade defi

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.279
As you can imagine, this was a huge blow to
the Japanese industry, who was used to competing

00:08:05.279 --> 00:08:06.279
with some help.

00:08:06.279 --> 00:08:07.279
And, what do you think the government did?

00:08:07.279 --> 00:08:08.279
It told them “Don’t worry, I’m here
to save you!"

00:08:08.279 --> 00:08:10.559
And then the Japanese government sank its
interest rates and began boosting credit like

00:08:10.559 --> 00:08:22.310
there was no tomorrow.

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:23.340
The result?

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:29.169
A huge debt spiral, banks distributing money
like crazy and a massive bubble in the real

00:08:29.169 --> 00:08:30.789
estate and financial markets.

00:08:30.789 --> 00:08:32.180
And, do you know what happened?

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:39.479
Well, in 1990, this entire house of cards
began to collapse...

00:08:39.479 --> 00:08:44.759
In Germany, however, as we also saw here at
VISUALPOLITIK, the government opted to maintain

00:08:44.759 --> 00:08:49.490
a strong Mark (their currency at that time),
which on one hand forced companies to compete

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:54.100
in productivity and innovation, but on the
other allowed them to buy foreign technology

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.279
and machines under better terms.

00:08:56.279 --> 00:09:01.320
But, my friends, there’s still a third…
and determinant difference.

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.690
Listen up.

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:09.750
(LOCKED WITHIN THEMSELVES)

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:12.630
Japan is the most elderly country in the world.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:18.740
Just over 25% of its population, that is,
1 in 4 Japanese are over 65; and for every

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:22.399
hour that passes, the country’s population
drops by 50 people.

00:09:22.399 --> 00:09:23.930
You heard that right.

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:28.740
In fact, the Japanese government expects the
country's population to fall below 100 million

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.870
people by the year 2050.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:34.560
And of course, the more elderly citizens there
are, the less young people, and the lower

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:35.560
population.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:37.550
This has clear consequences:

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:42.709
For one, more public spending on pensions
and health care, and for another, less labor.

00:09:42.709 --> 00:09:48.880
True, many other countries have this problem
but Japan’s case is by far the most serious.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:54.690
And this isn’t at all by chance.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:58.779
Japanese companies have compensated for all
the problems caused by the bad policies that

00:09:58.779 --> 00:10:02.570
we went over in this video, forcing their
employees to work for longer hours than in

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:06.560
other developed countries... which obviously,
leaves very little time to think about starting

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:07.560
a family.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.339
What’s more, while some countries, such
as Germany, have favored the arrival of immigrants,

00:10:11.339 --> 00:10:16.480
who tend to have higher population growth
rates, Japan has closed its doors.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:22.470
In fact, when international organizations
tell Japan that they have to open their borders

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:24.380
to immigration to face the aging problem…

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:27.300
the Japanese government has even said this:

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:31.600
(AUDIO: “Japan's finance minister tells
elderly they should 'hurry up and die' to

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:33.470
help reduce country's rising welfare bill”.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:34.470
Daily Mail.)

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.810
So my friends, these are the fundamental causes
that have led Japan to live in continuous

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:41.880
crisis; and the causes that have made the
German model clearly better than the Japanese

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:42.880
model.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:43.880
But now, it’s your turn.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:48.420
Do you think Japan will manage to overcome
this stagnation and permanent crisis period?

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:51.100
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

